* 2026-09-19 Imre Tabur <imre.tabur@mail.ee>
- setmy-info-external-ip.timer runs at half past every hour only, OnCalendar=*-*-* *:30:00 (12:30, 13:30, ...), not at the full hours as well; the OnActiveSec=1min run after installation and boot is kept. Man page updated.
- smi-external-ip takes an optional FILE argument and also writes the address there. The file is written as FILE.tmp and moved over FILE with mv, so a reader never sees a partial file, and it is not touched when no service gives an address, so it keeps the last known address instead of becoming empty. The address is still printed, so every timer run leaves it in the journal. An unwritable FILE exits 1.
- Added setmy-info-external-ip.timer and setmy-info-external-ip.service: the timer (OnCalendar=*-*-* *:00,30:00) starts the oneshot service every half hour, plus OnActiveSec=1min, once a minute after the timer is activated at installation and at every boot, so /var/opt/setmy.info/ip.txt is filled without waiting up to half an hour. The service runs /opt/setmy.info/bin/smi-external-ip /var/opt/setmy.info/ip.txt as root, after network-online.target.
- Installation the standard SMI way: base targets.cmake buildServiceScripts now also copies *.timer units, base etc.cmake installs both units to /opt/setmy.info/etc/systemd/system, rpm post.install.sh and deb postinst link them into /etc/systemd/system and run systemctl enable --now setmy-info-external-ip.timer; rpm post.uninstall.sh and deb postrm disable the timer and remove both links.
- smi-external-ip man page: FILE argument, TIMER section with the schedule and inspection commands, FILES, EXIT STATUS and SEE ALSO updated.
- Verified: against the real services the address is printed and written to the file; with every service failing the previous file content stays and the exit is 1; with the first service down the fallback address replaces the file; in a read only directory the exit is 1 and the old file stays. systemd-analyze verify accepts the timer and service, systemd-analyze calendar gives the next runs at :30, :00 and :30. The rebuilt RPM contains both units and the command, and its postinstall and postuninstall scriptlets carry the link, enable and disable lines.
- Added smi-external-ip: prints the external Internet IP address of the machine and nothing else, for use in scripts. Tries https://icanhazip.com, https://ifconfig.me and https://api.ipify.org in that order with curl -fsS --max-time 5 and prints the first valid answer; exits 1 with a message on standard error when no service gives an address.
- smi-external-ip: an answer counts only when it is non empty and made of hexadecimal digits, dots and colons, an IPv4 or IPv6 address. curl -f makes an HTTP error a failure, and the character check skips a service that answers 200 with other content, such as a captive portal login page, instead of printing that page as the address.
- Installed via base bin cmake, man page smi-external-ip(1) in the base module.
- Verified: against the real services prints the address with exit 0; with a stubbed curl the second service is used when the first is unreachable, an HTML page from the first is skipped and an IPv6 answer from the second is printed, and with all services failing the command exits 1 with the error message.

* 2026-09-18 Imre Tabur <imre.tabur@mail.ee>
- Added gbare: deletes a git branch locally with git branch -D and on origin with git push origin --delete. Added gbare.cmd Windows wrapper, gbare.1 man page (base module), and CMake installation rules for base bin.
- Added PKCS#12 export to the client certificate tools: pki.sh pkiExportPkcs12 PRIV_KEY_FILE CERT_FILE P12_FILE FRIENDLY_NAME [CA_CERT_FILE] bundles a key and its certificate, with the Intermediate CA as chain when given, into a .p12 file written mode 600 under the alias FRIENDLY_NAME. PKCS#12 is the one file browsers, Java keystores and .NET import; PEM key and certificate are two files. New CERT_PKCS12_SUFFIX=p12.
- The export password is read from SMI_PKI_PKCS12_PASSWORD with openssl -passout env:, not with -passout pass: as in the sketch, because a password on the command line is visible to every user in the process list. Without the variable OpenSSL asks on the terminal; with no terminal the command fails and writes no file.
- pki.sh pkiExportClientPkcs12 DIR CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN is the CA side form with the client file naming: <client-name>.client.priv.key, .client.crt and ca/<root-domain>.intermediate.crt into <client-name>.client.p12. pkiCreateClientCert now runs it as its last step, so creating a client certificate on the CA side also produces the bundle the client imports. pkiDoClientSigning deliberately does not export a bundle: when the client made its own request, its private key never reached the CA side, and the client makes the bundle itself with smi-pki-export-pkcs12.
- Added commands smi-pki-export-pkcs12 and smi-pki-export-client-pkcs12, installed via base bin cmake, with man pages; their usage message also names the password variable. smi-pki-create-client-cert man page documents the .client.p12 file, the password variable and that the command is interactive without it; the client man pages link the new commands.
- Verified in a scratch PKI directory with the gintra CA: smi-pki-create-client-cert writes bot2.client.p12 mode 600 containing the client certificate (clientAuth) and the Intermediate CA, plus the private key; a wrong password does not open it; keytool lists it as one PrivateKeyEntry with alias bot2. On the client side smi-pki-export-pkcs12 bundles the customer's own key with the received certificate and the Intermediate CA, two certificates, keytool alias acme-billing. Passing the .client.chain.crt file as CA_CERT_FILE puts the client certificate in twice, three certificates, so the man page says to pass the Intermediate CA certificate. Without the password variable and without a terminal the command exits 1 and writes no file; a missing key file exits 1 naming the file.

* 2026-09-17 Imre Tabur <imre.tabur@mail.ee>
- Added client certificates for mutual TLS to pki.sh; until now it could only issue server certificates. Flow: the client creates its own key and request, sends only the CSR, the CA side checks and signs it with the Intermediate CA and sends back a chain file. Client files are named <client-name>.client.{priv.key,pub.key,csr,crt,ext,chain.crt}, like the .intermediate. CA files, so a client name never collides with a server certificate in the same directory.
- pki.sh pkiClientExtensions: basicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE, keyUsage=critical,digitalSignature, extendedKeyUsage=clientAuth, subjectKeyIdentifier, authorityKeyIdentifier; no subjectAltName, the client is identified by its Common Name. digitalSignature alone because a TLS client only signs the handshake, which is also right for EC keys where keyEncipherment would not be. New tunable CLIENT_CERT_VALIDITY_DAYS=365.
- pki.sh pkiDoClientCertRequest DIR CLIENT_NAME: client side, 2048-bit key (mode 600), public key and CSR with CN=CLIENT_NAME and the other subject fields from the client's own subject.sh, so a customer names its own organization.
- pki.sh pkiVerifyClientCSR CSR_FILE CLIENT_NAME: the CSR self-signature must verify (the sender holds the private key) and its only Common Name must be exactly the agreed CLIENT_NAME, so a customer cannot request a certificate in another client's name. Extensions requested in the CSR are deliberately not checked, they are never used: openssl x509 -req copies no request extensions, every extension comes from the extension file.
- pki.sh pkiDoClientSigning DIR CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN: requires the Intermediate CA of ROOT_DOMAIN, runs pkiVerifyClientCSR on DIR/CLIENT_NAME.client.csr, signs it with the client extensions via pkiSignCSR, and writes CLIENT_NAME.client.chain.crt (certificate followed by the Intermediate CA certificate) to send back. ROOT_DOMAIN is required, a client name is not a host name to derive it from. pkiCreateClientCert DIR CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN does both sides at once for clients whose key the CA side creates, checking the Intermediate CA before generating anything.
- Added a command and a man page for each function, in the style of the other PKI commands: smi-pki-client-extensions, smi-pki-do-client-cert-request, smi-pki-verify-client-csr, smi-pki-do-client-signing, smi-pki-create-client-cert; installed via base bin cmake, man pages via the pki module. The request, signing and verify pages describe the mutual TLS workflow including nginx ssl_client_certificate and ssl_verify_client. smi-pki-ca-create SEE ALSO lists smi-pki-do-client-signing.
- Verified with the gintra CA in a scratch directory: a customer directory with its own subject.sh creates key and CSR; the CA side verifies and signs the copied CSR, issuer HAS Internal Intermediate CA, subject O=Acme Oy CN=acme-billing, CA:FALSE, keyUsage Digital Signature, clientAuth; openssl verify -purpose sslclient OK and -purpose sslserver fails; the chain file has two certificates. A CSR with the wrong Common Name is refused and no certificate written. A hostile CSR asking for CA:TRUE, serverAuth and DNS:setmy.info.gintra gets CA:FALSE, clientAuth only and no SAN. A CSR with a broken signature is refused by verify and by signing. A missing Intermediate CA creates no files; missing arguments print usage and exit 1. Real handshake with openssl s_server (setmy.info.gintra server certificate, -Verify with Root and Intermediate CA) and s_client: the acme-billing chain and the customer side key are accepted with verify return code 0, a client without certificate is refused with "certificate required", and the server certificate used as client certificate is refused with "unsuitable certificate purpose".
- Fixed smi-pki-create-domain-cert for a server more than one label below the CA root domain: pkiDoCASigning always took the root domain as the host name minus its first label, so with a CA created for gintra the certificate setmy.info.gintra looked for ca/info.gintra.intermediate.crt and failed with "Could not open file or uri for loading CA certificate". smi-pki-create-domain-cert and smi-pki-do-ca-signing take an optional third argument ROOT_DOMAIN, the root domain given to smi-pki-ca-create: smi-pki-create-domain-cert DIR setmy.info.gintra gintra. Without it the old derivation is used, so existing calls such as ldap.has.ee.gintra with a has.ee.gintra CA are unchanged.
- pki.sh: new pkiRootDomain (ROOT_DOMAIN when given, otherwise the domain minus its first label) and pkiRequireIntermediateCA (fails naming the missing intermediate key or certificate and pointing at the root domain argument). pkiDoCASigning and pkiCreateDomainCert accept ROOT_DOMAIN as third argument. pkiCreateDomainCert checks the Intermediate CA before pkiDoCertRequest, so a wrong root domain no longer leaves a generated server key, public key and CSR behind.
- smi-pki-create-domain-cert always exited 0, also when signing failed; it now exits with the status of pkiCreateDomainCert, and prints usage and exits 1 without DIRECTORY and DOMAIN_NAME.
- Verified in a scratch PKI directory: CA and Intermediate CA for gintra; setmy.info.gintra without a root domain exits 1 naming ca/info.gintra.intermediate.priv.key and writes no setmy.info.gintra files; with root domain gintra the certificate is issued by HAS Internal Intermediate CA with CA:FALSE, serverAuth and DNS:setmy.info.gintra, and openssl verify against the gintra Root CA and Intermediate CA is OK; the two step smi-pki-do-cert-request plus smi-pki-do-ca-signing with gintra verifies OK; ldap.has.ee.gintra without a root domain against a has.ee.gintra CA still verifies OK.
- Man pages smi-pki-create-domain-cert (ROOT_DOMAIN argument, ARGUMENTS, EXAMPLES, EXIT STATUS) and smi-pki-do-ca-signing (ROOT_DOMAIN argument) updated.
- Added lib/smi-incoming-deploy.sh (base module): one generic deployment plugin for smi-incoming-deploy instead of a script per deployment. It is started through symbolic links in /opt/setmy.info/lib/incoming and takes its pattern from the link name without .sh; a package whose file name contains the pattern is unpacked with tar xvzf into /usr/share/nginx (created when missing), any other package is ignored with exit 0. A new deployment is only a new link.
- smi-incoming-deploy.sh compares the pattern with the package file name only, not the whole path, so a directory name containing the pattern never selects a package. A failed tar prints an error and exits 1 instead of printing Success and exiting 0; smi-incoming-deploy runs with set -e, so a broken package stops the run and the setmy-info-deploy service reports the failure.
- The first link, /opt/setmy.info/lib/incoming/angular-start-project.sh -> /opt/setmy.info/lib/smi-incoming-deploy.sh, is created the standard SMI way, like bin/smi-test: rpm post.install.sh and deb postinst create lib/incoming with mkdir -p and the link with ln -f -s, rpm post.uninstall.sh and deb postrm remove it with rm -f. The link is not a packaged file. base lib.cmake installs only the script, as a program (755): the link target must be executable because smi-incoming-deploy runs only executable plugins. Verified: the RPM lists lib/smi-incoming-deploy.sh 755 and no lib/incoming entry, its postinstall and postuninstall scriptlets carry the link lines, and those lines run against a scratch root create a link resolving to the executable script and remove it again.
- Verified through the real smi-incoming-deploy loop with paths redirected to a scratch directory: angular-start-project-1.0.0.tar.gz unpacked, other-app.tar.gz ignored, a pattern present only in the directory path ignored, a corrupt matching package exits 1 with an error.
- smi-incoming-deploy.sh removes the package from the incoming directory after a successful unpack. Before, the package stayed, so the setmy-info-deploy.path unit (PathExistsGlob on incoming/*.tar.gz) started the deployment again after every run until systemd's start limit stopped it, unpacking the same package over and over. Removal is done by the plugin, not by smi-incoming-deploy, because only the plugin knows it handled the package: a package no link matches stays for the link that will handle it, and a package that fails to unpack stays for inspection with exit 1. A failed rm prints an error and exits 1. When two links match the same package the first deploys and removes it and the second only prints the existing "matched, but file was not found" warning.
- Verified through the real smi-incoming-deploy loop with paths redirected to a scratch directory: a matching package is unpacked and removed while a non matching one stays; with two overlapping links the package is deployed once, removed, and the second link warns; a corrupt package stays; a package in a read only incoming directory is unpacked, reported as not removable, and stays.
- smi-incoming-deploy man page: new PLUGINS section on the generic script, the link name as pattern, the installed angular-start-project.sh link, how to add a link, that the pattern matches anywhere in the file name, that a deployed package is removed while failed and unmatched ones stay, and to move a package into the incoming directory only when its upload is complete; FILES lists the generic script and /usr/share/nginx; EXIT STATUS lists a plugin failure.
- Added remote.sh library (base module): the SSH connection handling shared by smi-upload and smi-remote-exec, so neither command repeats it. Reads SMI_REMOTE_HOST (no default), SMI_REMOTE_PORT (default 22), SMI_REMOTE_USER (default the current login name) and SMI_REMOTE_PASSWORD (default empty) into REMOTE_HOST, REMOTE_PORT, REMOTE_USER and REMOTE_PASSWORD. Functions: remoteParseServer ([USER@]HOST[:PORT] override), remoteRequireHost, remotePreparePassword, remoteDestination, remoteQuoteArgument and remoteQuoteCommand. One set of variables serves an upload and the remote commands that follow it on the same host.
- remote.sh: the password is given to sshpass through the SSHPASS environment variable (sshpass -e), never with sshpass -p, so it is not readable from the process list. With no password set, scp and ssh run without sshpass and key based authentication works; sshpass is only required when a password is actually used, and a missing sshpass is reported with a clear message and exit 1.
- remote.sh: StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new instead of no. An unknown host is accepted without the interactive question, so unattended use works, but a host key that changed after it was stored is refused rather than silently trusted. Needs OpenSSH 7.6 or newer, which Rocky Linux 9 and 10, Fedora and FreeBSD all have.
- Added smi-upload: uploads one local file to a path on a remote host with scp. smi-upload SOURCE_FILE DESTINATION_FILE [[USER@]HOST[:PORT]]. Connection from the SMI_REMOTE_* variables, the same shape as smi-sendmail and its SMI_SMTP_* variables; the optional third argument overrides user, host and port for one call. It is the first scp based command in the project, smi-download uses curl and smi-sync uses rsync. Exits 1 on wrong usage, a missing host or a missing source file before any connection is made; otherwise the exit status of scp or sshpass is passed through (sshpass 5 is a wrong password, 6 an unknown host key), with a one line success or failure message. Paths with spaces are quoted through.
- Added smi-remote-exec: runs a command on the remote host over ssh, used like sudo. smi-remote-exec COMMAND [ARGUMENT ...], every argument belongs to the remote command, the command has no options of its own and the connection comes only from the SMI_REMOTE_* variables. Example: smi-remote-exec mv '~/deployments/a.tar.gz' /var/opt/a/deploy/a.tar.gz.
- smi-remote-exec: ssh joins its arguments into one string that the remote shell splits again, so an argument with a space would become two and characters such as $ * ; would be interpreted remotely. Every argument is now single quoted for the remote shell (embedded single quotes escaped as '\'') so the remote command receives exactly the local argument list: spaces, quotes, $, *, ;, empty arguments and newlines verified one to one. Shell syntax on the remote host is used explicitly with smi-remote-exec sh -c '...'.
- smi-remote-exec: an argument that is exactly ~ or starts with ~/ keeps the tilde unquoted, so it expands to the remote user's home, not literally. The local shell expands an unquoted ~ before the command sees it, so the man page tells to write '~/deployments/...' quoted; relative paths also resolve against the remote home.
- smi-remote-exec: prints nothing of its own and passes standard input, standard output, standard error and the remote command's exit status through (ssh 255 on a connection failure), so it works in pipelines and scripts. Exits 1 on wrong usage, a missing host or a missing sshpass. No terminal is allocated, documented: a remote sudo needs a NOPASSWD rule, and a read loop should redirect stdin from /dev/null.
- Verified with a stub ssh that evaluates the received command string with sh under a different HOME, and stub scp and sshpass: argument round trip, tilde expansion, the mv example, exit status 7 pass through, piped stdin, password and port handling, upload with shared variables and the third argument override.
- Installed smi-upload and smi-remote-exec via base bin cmake and remote.sh via base lib cmake. Added the smi-upload and smi-remote-exec man pages (base module), linked each other in SEE ALSO, and linked smi-upload from the smi-download man page.

* 2026-09-09 Imre Tabur <imre.tabur@mail.ee>
- smi-backup: the dotfiles archive was written for a Debian home only. Rocky Linux and Fedora have no ~/.profile, they have ~/.bash_profile, so the login shell configuration of those machines was never backed up while tar reported the missing ~/.profile on every run. backupFiles now packs ~/.profile, ~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login, ~/.bashrc and ~/.bash_logout, filtering the list down to the files the machine actually has before calling tar, so both home shapes are backed up whole and neither reports a missing file. --ignore-failed-read is gone with the reason for it; an unreadable file that does exist fails the backup again. Man page updated.
- sbcl package: the Quicklisp step stopped at "Press Enter to continue." and waited for a keypress, so installing SBCL was not unattended. ql:add-to-init-file is now called inside ql-util:without-prompting, Quicklisp's own switch for that prompt, and it appends the loader to ~/.sbclrc without asking. sbcl also runs with --disable-debugger so any other surprise ends the process with an error instead of leaving it waiting on a terminal that is not there.
- libreoffice package: install used "dnf localinstall", which dnf5 (Fedora 41 and newer) removed, so installing LibreOffice failed on Fedora. Uses "dnf install" with the local .rpm paths, which both dnf4 on Rocky Linux and dnf5 accept.

* 2026-09-08 Imre Tabur <imre.tabur@mail.ee>
- smi-backup: also backs up ~/.config, ~/.local/share/TelegramDesktop and the shell login files ~/.profile and ~/.bashrc. The two dotfiles go into a single dotfiles.tar.gz via a new backupFiles helper that groups several home files into one archive and skips the missing ones. ~/.config is packed with the browser and Electron cache directories (Cache, Code Cache, GPUCache, Service Worker, Crashpad and similar) excluded, so a 1.5G config of mostly disposable cache becomes a small archive while settings and messaging data such as Signal under ~/.config/Signal are kept. tar exit 1, a file changing or vanishing while read (common for a running application's config), is now a warning rather than aborting the backup; exit 2 and above still fail.
- Refactored pki.sh into small single-purpose file-based functions. Low level: pkiGeneratePrivateKey (RSA size a parameter, CA_KEY_BITS 4096 default, SERVER_KEY_BITS 2048), pkiGeneratePublicKey, pkiCreateCSR (optional SAN via -addext), pkiCreateSelfSignedCertificate, pkiSignCSR (only ever reads the CA key file, never creates CA key material). Extension writers: pkiRootCaExtensions, pkiIntermediateCaExtensions, pkiServerExtensions. Everything passes keys, CSRs, certs and extension files as file names; no PKI material is held in shell variables; each openssl call fails the function immediately. Process substitution replaced by generated .ext files so each step is inspectable and testable.
- Introduced a two level CA: new pkiStartIntermediateCA creates a 4096-bit Intermediate CA (CA:TRUE,pathlen:0, keyCertSign/cRLSign, SKI, AKI) signed by the Root CA, never self-signed, stored as <root-domain>.intermediate.{priv.key,pub.key,csr,crt} in the ca directory. pkiStartCA now gives the Root CA a descriptive Common Name (HAS Internal Root CA) instead of intranet.<domain> and a 4096-bit key. pkiDoCASigning now signs server CSRs with the Intermediate CA, not the Root CA. Chain is Root CA -> Intermediate CA -> Server; verified with openssl verify.
- Server certificates now get CA:FALSE, critical keyUsage digitalSignature,keyEncipherment, extendedKeyUsage=serverAuth and subjectAltName=DNS:<domain> (exact host, no wildcard); the SAN, not the Common Name, identifies the host. Removed the broken -config openssl.cfg and -days from the CSR step, and the extra www SAN.
- Quoted every file path and openssl argument. Root and intermediate keys, and server keys, are written mode 600.
- smi-pki-ca-create creates the Root CA and, only when the optional third argument is true/yes/1, the Intermediate CA too; by default it creates the Root CA alone (one thing). The Intermediate CA can also be made separately with smi-pki-start-intermediate-ca. smi-pki-create-domain-cert signs through the Intermediate CA. Rewrote both man pages.
- Added a CLI binary for every PKI library function so a CA and certificates can be built step by step, each step inspectable: smi-pki-generate-private-key, smi-pki-generate-public-key, smi-pki-create-csr, smi-pki-create-self-signed-certificate, smi-pki-sign-csr, smi-pki-root-ca-extensions, smi-pki-intermediate-ca-extensions, smi-pki-server-extensions, smi-pki-subject-string, smi-pki-start-ca, smi-pki-start-intermediate-ca, smi-pki-do-cert-request, smi-pki-do-ca-signing. Each is a thin wrapper with a usage guard, installed via base bin cmake, with a man page.
- Fixed pki cmake module: buildPKIMan was defined but never wired into build (pki dependencies.cmake was empty), so the PKI man pages, including the pre-existing smi-pki-ca-create and smi-pki-create-domain-cert, were never installed. Wired it in; all PKI man pages now install.

